Priya, Gwen —

I pulled the order data rather than the lead data, since orders are the thing we bank. Method: all new and reactivated account orders from 2026-04-16 (day after the show closed) through 2026-05-07, plus existing-account orders containing SKUs sampled at the show, cross-checked against the CRM campaign_source field.

Findings:

1. campaign_source was populated on 6.3% of post-show orders. Orders we can actually attribute to the April show total $241K.

2. The $3.2M pipeline figure is the sum of the lead sheets collected at the booth. The interest level is self-reported by the person standing at the booth, there is no follow-up disposition recorded on 71% of the sheets, and none of it links to an order record unless a rep happens to fill in campaign_source at order entry — which, per the above, essentially no one does.

3. To be precise about what this does and does not mean: That is not a statement that the show produced only $241K. It is a statement that we cannot know. The tracking has to be fixed before we spend another $410K.

Recommendation for FY2027 budgeting: treat show ROI as unmeasured rather than either $3.2M or $241K. If we want a defensible number next year, the fix is mechanical — campaign_source becomes a required field at order entry for 90 days post-show, lead sheets get entered with a disposition workflow, and we agree the definition of "attributable" before the show, not after.

Happy to walk through the pull line by line.

Marcus

